  Analysis and agreed design (implementation is separately selected):
    Current user decisions:
    - Assertion programs should use the actual production precompile/shader-asset and default binary-cache
      pipeline. Replace checker-specific source compilation/cache plumbing through that existing system;
      do not add a special test executable cache. Inventory generated predicate/transform bodies and move
      their finite authored variants into declared build inputs, with scenario values supplied as data.
      Production binary caching and native-owner retention are distinct: cached bytes alone do not prove
      that repeated native program construction has been eliminated. The approved ownership arrangement
      prepares each declared checker through the production shader system, retains its production shader
      owner for the GPU fixture's physical context lifetime, explicitly initializes each assertion's
      inputs/bindings/uniforms/results, and disposes those owners before context destruction. Use the
      existing production lifecycle; no additional test-specific cache or retention service. Preserve
      explicit fresh-creation/fault-injection subjects.
    - Compact expectations are approved, preserving independent numerical evidence and exact domains.
    - Workgroup reductions are approved, preserving full-domain checks, exact counters, earliest failures,
      negative witnesses and the required numerical precision/addition order.
    - Batched observations/results are deferred as a later decision. The batching opportunities below
      remain analysis candidates, not approved implementation or prerequisites for the selected changes.
    - No numerical performance requirement applies for now. Discuss the actual resulting metrics with
      the user at each implementation subphase's closeout; the user accepts or rejects that work then.
      Report the final combined metrics in 10.10 as well. Do not substitute a predetermined percentage,
      time limit or automatic speedup verdict for that review, or run full suites for each checkpoint.
