    Owning-boundary design, subject to the current user decisions above:
    1. Deferred batching candidate: explicit within-test observation batches would own
       one capture per selected source/version, independent result slots and reusable initialized scratch.
       Enqueue compatible checks on the owning GL thread, then perform one bounded combined result read.
       Finish the batch before the caller needs its CPU result, mutates/retires its source or crosses a
       required lifecycle boundary. Retained history still owns its copy. No cross-case mutable targets.
       Keep the synchronous convenience route through that same implementation, not duplicate dispatchers.
    2. Approved workgroup reductions: reduce inside each workgroup and publish partial/aggregate counters instead of globally contending
       per successful component. Compute only requested facts while retaining full-domain visited counts,
       completion magic, earliest failure and negative witnesses. Preserve exact integer/bit/nonfinite
       semantics and required ordered-float/double reductions; do not change tolerances or numeric grouping.
    3. Approved shader integration and retention: use the production precompiled shader asset, default binary-cache and shader-owner systems for
       checker programs. Replace GpuFixtureProgram's hand-built source compilation and checker-specific
       TestShaderCache path coherently with declared assets and consuming helpers. Retain the production
       shader owners for the GPU fixture's physical context lifetime; no additional test cache or retention
       service. Initialize inputs/uniforms/bindings/results for each assertion and dispose those owners
       before context destruction. Fault-injection/creation/cache subjects retain their required fresh operations and
       policy scopes; no raw-name globals, subject-program sharing or implicit cross-thread access.
    4. Approved compact expectations: add constant-vector and authored row/region/coordinate tables;
       the revised design below determines their focused observation owner. Select
       independent predicates for regular patterns and keep necessary CPU numerical arrays. Upload retained
       references once per unchanged authored owner/domain, preserve exact selected masks and double bounds.
       Avoid per-value shader-source variants, full sparse-image expansion and byte decode round trips.
    5. Deferred batching-dependent caller migration: water/local observation consumers and surface Frame/Energy/WaitObservations could migrate together so
       repeated facts from one unchanged output share a capture/result. Share immutable catalogs and
       authored input bytes; each case still owns/reset its native subjects. Keep worker/frame milestones,
       cache opt-outs, shader-operation matrices and representative full runtime chains. The subsequent audit below establishes consolidation candidates; remove repeated execution only
       through 10.9.3's explicit equivalent-coverage mapping. CPU atmosphere orchestration seams and fewer GC checkpoints are
       separate candidates requiring exact real-coverage mappings, not assumed implementation savings.
    - Expected benefit: high confidence in fewer allocations/copies, uploads, atomic operations and CPU/GPU
      rendezvous; low confidence in an absolute suite-time prediction before focused measurement. There
      is no measured claim that any proposed change saves a specific number of seconds. The subsequent audit identifies overlapping native/runtime link matrices; retain assignment coverage
      while consolidating proven duplicate execution through 10.9.3.
      Phase 11's legacy-to-production submission migration remains separate and unimplemented.

